<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarnOn>3390;$(WarnOn)</WarnOn>
    <!-- 0.1.11 (2026-07-22): the Phase 94 pilot-5 lenient wave (c5e7863) landed in
         Column.fs + DataFrame.fs AFTER 0.1.10 was packed, so the feed's 0.1.10
         bytes predated the feature whose own commit message names that version.
         Additive — values-only columns, flat logical/comparison spellings, epoch
         timestamps — so consumers repin without source changes. Advancing rather
         than repacking 0.1.10 in place: this is the one package every domain
         consumes, so a same-version swap would give each machine a different
         contract depending only on when it last packed. -->
    <!-- 0.2.1 (2026-07-31): fuaran#665 — `Fuaran.Core.Row` + `RowCodec` (typed grid/chart
         row-source payload; the rows slot leaves the residual-"<opaque>" boundary).
         Additive — new type + module in Fuaran.Core.Wire; no existing surface moved. -->
    <!-- 0.3.0 (2026-08-18): Phase 101 — the relational algebra's closed-set asymmetries.
         `Transform` gains Intersect/Except; `JoinKind` Semi/Anti; `AggFn` CountDistinct;
         `WindowFn` DenseRank/CompetitionRank/NTile/CumulMax/CumulMin/RollingSum; `ScalarFn`
         Sqrt/Least/Greatest/IndexOf. Additive — every pre-existing pipeline encodes to
         byte-identical wire (the sole new field, a window step's "n", is emitted only for
         NTile), so consumers repin without source changes. MINOR rather than patch because
         STABILITY.md pins "adding a step kind is minor", and because an F# consumer that
         matches these DUs exhaustively will see new incomplete-match warnings. -->
    <!-- 0.4.0 (2026-08-20): `Fuaran.Core.Idl` joins the packed set — the IDL engine was
         IsPackable=false from its Phase 316 spike, so no domain workspace could consume it.
         Adding a package to the repo's published set is a contract change even though no
         existing package's surface moved: `dotnet pack Fuaran.Core.slnx` now emits a tenth
         nupkg, and a consumer that pins 0.4.0 gets an id that did not exist at 0.3.0.
         MINOR rather than patch on those grounds; purely additive for the nine existing
         packages, so consumers repin without source changes. See DECISIONS.md D14. -->
    <!-- 0.5.0 (2026-08-21): `Hash` gains the pinned pure SHA-256 — `sha256Hex` over a string,
         `sha256Bytes` / `sha256HexOfBytes` over arbitrary bytes, and the `utf8Bytes` encoder they
         hash through. Purely additive: `fnv1a` and `foldSep` are byte-identical and merely moved
         into their own file, so every stored content hash is unchanged and consumers repin without
         source changes. MINOR rather than patch because a new public function in a shipped module
         is a contract a consumer can pin against, and because this reverses a stated posture —
         STABILITY.md previously said no cryptographic hash ships in Core. See DECISIONS.md D15. -->
    <!-- 0.6.0 (2026-08-21): every FNV-1a in the spine routes its multiply through a split-half
         32-bit form so it computes true 32-bit FNV-1a on BOTH pipelines. Six implementations, not
         one: the canonical `Hash.fnv1a`, `OpStream.defaultHash` (the CHAIN hash), `Column`'s
         `Schema.fingerprint`, and three duplicates now deleted in favour of the canonical one.
         No public surface changed and
         .NET values are byte-for-byte unchanged — the pinned vectors enforce that — so a .NET
         consumer repins with no source change and no persisted digest moves. MINOR rather than
         patch because STABILITY.md makes these functions' VALUES the contract, and under Fable
         every value changes: the pre-0.6.0 transpiled multiply overflowed 2^53 and was not FNV-1a.
         A patch bump would tell a Fable consumer nothing observable moved, which is false. See
         DECISIONS.md D16 and the "Digest changes" entry in STABILITY.md. -->
    <!-- 0.7.0 (2026-08-21): the null-tolerant read. `Fuaran.Core.Wire.NullPolicy` +
         `Json.parseDetailedWithPolicy` / `parseWithPolicy` / `parseTolerantOfNull*` and
         `Decode.parseTolerantOfNull`, so a consumer can read a foreign document that spells
         absent members `null` without the wire model gaining one; plus the
         `Conformance.WireNullTolerance` vector family that pins it. Purely additive — the
         strict policy is the default, every pre-existing entry point is byte-identical under
         it (same errors, positions and messages), and nothing on the encode path moved, so
         consumers repin without source changes. MINOR rather than patch because a new public
         type and new public functions in a shipped module are contracts a consumer can pin
         against, and because a consumer matching `NullPolicy` is now possible at all. -->
    <!-- 0.8.0 (2026-08-21): the IDL engine splits — `Fuaran.Core.Idl` keeps the model, the codec,
         the sampler, the `idl.json` artifact and the sanitisation floor, and is now Fable-gated;
         a new `Fuaran.Core.Idl.Codegen` package takes the source emitters, `CodegenError`, the
         codegen trust boundary and the diff classifier. BREAKING for a consumer of
         `Fuaran.Core.Idl` 0.4.0-0.6.0 that called `Gen`, `Trust` or `Diff`: those types are still
         `Fuaran.Core.Idl.*` and still compile unchanged, but they arrive from a second package now,
         so the fix is one added PackageReference and no source edit. `Gen.sampleNodes` is the one
         genuine source change — it is `Sample.sampleNodes`, and it moved the other way, INTO the
         model half. MINOR rather than major under the pre-1.0 policy at the head of STABILITY.md.
         Additive for the nine non-IDL packages, which repin with no change at all. Eleven nupkgs
         where 0.7.0 emitted ten. See DECISIONS.md D17 and STABILITY.md "The IDL engine — two
         packages, two promises". -->
    <!-- 0.9.0 (2026-08-21): `Fuaran.Core.DataFrame` gains the typed delta representation for the
         column layer — `TableDelta` / `RowSetDelta` / `RowRef` / `RowChange` / `DeltaDefect` /
         `RowIdentity<'Id>`, the `Delta` algebra (associative composition, `FullRefresh` absorbing,
         validation that refuses a delta whole) and `DeltaCodec`. Purely additive: no existing type,
         function or wire byte moved, and `DataFrame`'s three new public helpers (`cellToken` /
         `rowToken` / `rowTokenString`) merely expose the canonicalisation `groupKey` already used,
         so consumers repin without source changes. MINOR rather than patch because new public types
         and modules in a shipped package are a contract a consumer can pin against. See DECISIONS.md
         D18. -->
    <!-- 0.10.0 (2026-08-21): Phase 100 — the fold-confluence pack. `Fuaran.Core.OpStream.Dag` gains
         `Dag.reconcileMany` (the N-lane generalisation of `reconcile`, identical to it at N = 2),
         and `Fuaran.Core.Conformance` gains the `FoldConfluence` module — `laneFoldLaws` /
         `certifyFold` plus the pieces a domain drives directly when investigating a counterexample
         (`foldOnce`, `shrinkLanes`, `arrivalOrders`, `canonicalConflictReport`, `renderLanes`) and
         the two new public types `LaneFoldOutcome` / `LaneGen`. Purely additive: no existing
         function's behaviour, signature or emitted bytes moved, so consumers repin without source
         changes. MINOR rather than patch because new public types and functions in shipped packages
         are a contract a consumer can pin against — the same grounds as 0.5.0. See STABILITY.md
         "Fold-confluence pack: N-lane arrival-order invariance". -->
    <!-- 0.11.0 (2026-08-21): `Fuaran.Core.DataFrame` gains the incremental evaluation seam —
         `Incremental` (`plan` / `prime` / `refresh` / `evalDelta` and their embedded-source
         variants) over the new `StepIncrementality` / `IncrementalStrategy` / `IncrementalPlan` /
         `Recompute` / `RecomputeFootprint` / `IncrementalEval` / `FallBackReason` types, plus the
         `IncrementalDelta` equivalence family in `Fuaran.Core.Conformance`. `DataFrame` gains four
         wrappers over its own private primitives (`evalExprInRow` / `aggregateCells` /
         `aggregateType` / `inferCellType`) so the incremental path computes through the reference
         evaluator rather than a copy of it. Purely additive: no existing type, function, wire byte
         or evaluation result moved, so consumers repin without source changes. MINOR rather than
         patch because new public types and a new module in a shipped package are a contract a
         consumer can pin against. Note the footprint type is `RecomputeFootprint`, not
         `Footprint` — `Fuaran.Core.Ops` already publishes a `Fuaran.Core.Footprint` (the op-script
         address set), and two same-named types in one namespace across two packages is a
         collision a consumer opening both would hit. See DECISIONS.md D19 and STABILITY.md
         "Incremental column-layer evaluation". -->
    <!-- 0.12.0 (2026-08-21): `Fuaran.Core.Conformance`'s `ConfRng.intBelow` draws from the
         HIGH-ORDER bits by rejection instead of reducing `v % n`. The state is an LCG taken mod
         2^32, in which bit `k` has period 2^(k+1), so a modulo by a small `n` read the weakest
         bits in the word — and, worse, read them IN PHASE, so generators drawn consecutively off
         one stream chose in lockstep rather than independently. Measured while building the
         fold-confluence pack: a three-lane generator halted on 150 of 150 trials and the fold law
         never executed, which the pack's own vacuity guard reported. The replacement takes the top
         `bitWidth (n - 1)` bits and redraws an out-of-range candidate: only full-period bits, and
         exactly uniform rather than modulo-biased. Built from shifts and comparisons alone — no
         `uint64`, no 32-bit multiply — so the kit stays Fable-value-identical (the `Hash.fs`
         constraint 0.6.0 was cut for).
         NOT additive, unlike every bump since 0.5.0: no signature moves, but every value
         `intBelow` / `choose` / `shuffle` emits does, so a consumer pinning a seed sees different
         generated data and a law that certified green over one sample is certifying over another.
         That is the whole point — a weak generator in a conformance kit does not produce failures,
         it produces false assurance — but it means repinning is a READ, not a version swap. MINOR
         rather than patch on the same grounds as 0.6.0: this package's contract is its VALUES, and
         pre-1.0 MINOR is the lane that says look before you repin. See STABILITY.md
         "Fold-confluence pack: N-lane arrival-order invariance". -->
    <!-- 0.13.0 (2026-08-26): `Fuaran.Core.Idl.Codegen` models the NON-FINITE float. This
         package's declared contract IS THE SHAPE OF WHAT IT EMITS, so all four backends move
         together: the F# encoder routes every `TFloat` slot through a new `encFloat` helper
         that emits the WIRE_FORMAT §5 sentinel string (`"NaN"` / `"Infinity"` /
         `"-Infinity"`) for a non-finite and `JFloat` otherwise; the F# and TypeScript
         decoders read those three back AT A FLOAT SLOT per §7; and the JSON-schema leg emits
         `anyOf [ number, enum [the three] ]` there. `TInt` is untouched in all four — §7
         truncates at the float slot, so an integer slot still refuses a sentinel, and the
         corpus's two integer controls pin that.
         This does NOT overturn the wire model's stated no-non-finite-float posture: the
         sentinel is a JSON string, which `JVal` carries perfectly, and `Wire.tryRender`'s
         refusal of a non-finite `JFloat` is unchanged — building the `JStr` in the generated
         encoder is precisely what keeps the emitted value renderable by the guarded path.
         Additive to the generated layer's ACCEPT set (a document that decodes today still
         decodes) and byte-neutral on emission, since `Canon.render` already spelled a
         non-finite `JFloat` the same way. MINOR rather than patch because the emitted shape
         is this package's contract and a consumer's committed generated copy moves with it —
         `fuaran-dotnet`'s byte-pinned `src/Fuaran.UI/Generated.fs` is regenerated in the same
         change-set. Also fixes the corpus-certification leg's own reader: `IdlSchemaTests`
         parsed fixtures at `JsonDocument`'s default 64-level cap, below the 256 §21 pins, so
         the §21 at-the-limit node fixture failed on the READER rather than on the schema.
         See fuaran#1063. -->
    <!-- 0.14.0 (2026-08-26): `Gen.jsonSchema` emits a GENERIC union once per INSTANTIATED
         type argument, not once type-erased. JSON Schema has no type parameters, which is
         why the leg emitted a generic union's `'T`-typed fields as the permissive `{}`; but
         a schema needs no type parameters to state the INSTANTIATIONS, because the IDL names
         every one of them at the slot it is used from. So `Binding<'T>` now emits as
         `Binding_float` / `Binding_int` / `Binding_str` / `Binding_list_SelectOption` / …,
         each with its `Static.value` and three `defaultValue`s constrained by the element
         type — and `Local.initialFrom` pointing back at its OWN instantiation, which is the
         polymorphic recursion the IDL declares.
         The cost of the erased form was measured rather than supposed (fuaran#1064): a
         `Binding<float>` slot admitted a JSON boolean and a `Binding<int>` slot admitted a
         §7 non-finite sentinel, both of which the decoder refuses, so the published schema
         could not state a rule it was perfectly able to express.
         MINOR on the 0.13.0 precedent directly above — the emitted shape IS this package's
         contract — and a fresh version rather than a repack, because 0.13.0 is tagged and
         published, so re-packing that slot would make one machine's 0.13.0 differ from the
         world's. `Fuaran.Core.Idl.Codegen` is the only package whose delivered content moves;
         the other nine `Fuaran.Core.*` are byte-unchanged and carry the new stamp only.
         See fuaran#1068. -->
    <!-- 0.15.0 (2026-08-27): the codegen backends learn the EMPTY LIST as an omit-when-default
         value. Before this, `omit <field> (TList _) (VList [])` was an unrenderable default:
         `fsDefaultLit` / `tsDefaultLit` returned `None`, and the documented consequence of that
         is a silent FALLBACK TO ALWAYS-EMIT — so the declaration read as "omitted when empty"
         and emitted `"srcSet":[]` on every node that did not use the slot. A default the model
         accepts and the generator quietly ignores is worse than one it refuses.
         Four one-line additions, each mirroring the enum/nullary-union case beside it:
         `fsDefaultLit` and `defaultExpr` render `[]`; `tsDefaultLit` renders `[]` so an absent
         list decodes to the empty ARRAY rather than to `undefined` (the missing-list-field
         decode class); `tsIsDefault` renders `.length === 0`, because a JS array compares by
         reference. Plus one that is NOT a mirror and is the interesting half: the F# encoder's
         omit TEST emits `List.isEmpty s.X`, never `s.X = []`. That is Phase 691's union lesson
         reaching lists — an element type whose fields reach a closure (a `Binding` does) carries
         no equality, so the equality form does not COMPILE for the first list this feature was
         built for. Found by the compiler at the first regeneration, not reasoned out.
         MINOR on the 0.14.0 precedent directly above — the emitted shape IS this package's
         contract — and a fresh version rather than a repack, because v0.14.0 is tagged, so
         re-packing that slot would make one machine's 0.14.0 emit source the world's does not.
         `Fuaran.Core.Idl.Codegen` is the only package whose delivered content moves; the other
         nine `Fuaran.Core.*` are byte-unchanged and carry the new stamp only. Purely additive:
         every default shape that rendered before renders identically, and no existing IDL
         declares a list default, so no emitted artefact in the estate changes but the one this
         phase adds. See fuaran#1080. -->
    <!-- 0.16.0 (2026-08-30): fuaran-core#108/#109 — the declared WIRE SHAPE. `Idl` gains a
         `Wire: WireShape` slot (discriminator key + node-envelope nesting); the interpreter,
         the generated F#/TS legs and the schema all derive from it, and both readiness-spike
         vocabularies now certify their corpora in their NATIVE shape with no adapter. MINOR,
         and genuinely BREAKING at compile time: widening the `Idl` record fails every
         full-literal construction (FS0764) until it declares `Wire = WireShape.Default` — the
         one-line, behaviour-identical fix. Every WIRE byte is unchanged for default-shape
         declarations (the drift guards over the three committed generated modules and the
         artifact prove it), so no corpus, snapshot or emitted artefact moves. A fresh version
         rather than a repack on the standing grounds: v0.15.0 is tagged, and a same-version
         swap of a compile-breaking contract is the packall-incremental skew defect by
         construction. -->
    <!-- 0.17.0 (2026-08-30): fuaran-core#111 — declarable KEY ORDER, the wire shape's third
         axis and the readiness spikes' finding 3. `WireShape` gains `KeyOrder` (Sorted, the
         default | Declared — declaration-order canonical form via the new `Canon.renderOrdered`);
         the interpreter and the generated F#/TS legs derive from it, and both spike corpora now
         certify in their OWN byte order. MINOR and compile-breaking for `WireShape` literals
         (they add `KeyOrder = KeyOrder.Sorted`, or move to `{ WireShape.Default with … }`);
         every default-shape wire byte and generated artefact is unchanged. Supersedes 0.16.0
         within the same working day — 0.16.0 was packed to the local feed only, never tagged or
         published, and widening the same record twice across two unreleased versions is cheaper
         for every consumer than adopting it twice. -->
    <Version>0.20.0</Version>
    <Authors>Andrew J. Willshire</Authors>
    <Company>Diametrical Ltd</Company>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <Copyright>Copyright 2026 Diametrical Ltd</Copyright>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Fuaran-Core/fuaran-core</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Fuaran-Core/fuaran-core</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Fable source distribution. Every packable
       Core project ships its F# source + .fsproj under `fable/` in the package, so a
       Fable-compiled consumer (the Fuaran.UI client) compiles Core from source — alongside
       the `lib/` DLL for .NET. The standard dual-pack convention (Feliz / Thoth). The internal
       ProjectReferences become nuspec dependencies on pack; the dependency packages ship their
       own `fable/` source, so Fable resolves the whole Core graph transitively. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' == 'true'">
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildProjectFile)" PackagePath="fable\" />
    <Content Include="*.fs" PackagePath="fable\" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
